@@@@ show-me-paxos-sequence
situation: show-me
topic: Paxos: prepare, promise and accept
voice: plain
level: hard
expect: {"visual":{"kind":"mermaid","diagram":["sequenceDiagram"]},"must":["prepare\\(1\\)","accept\\(1, X\\)"]}
@@ tutor
Single-decree Paxos chooses one value in two phases. Phase 1: the proposer picks a proposal number n and sends prepare(n) to the acceptors. An acceptor that has not promised a higher number replies with a promise, reporting any proposal it has already accepted, and from then on ignores proposals numbered below n. Phase 2: once a majority has promised, the proposer sends accept(n, v), where v is the value of the highest-numbered proposal reported in the promises, or its own value if none was reported. An acceptor accepts unless it has since promised a higher number. The value is chosen once a majority has accepted it.

An example with three acceptors, where A3 is unreachable and the proposer uses n = 1 and wants value X: A1 and A2 promise, neither having accepted anything, so the proposer sends accept(1, X), both accept, and X is chosen by 2 of 3.

<note>Paxos. Phase 1: prepare(n) → promise (never accept below n; here is what I have accepted). Phase 2: accept(n, v) with v taken from the highest-numbered accepted proposal reported, else the proposer's own. Chosen when a majority accepts.</note>

<continue/>
@@ learner showme
@@ reply
```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant P as Proposer
    participant A1 as Acceptor A1
    participant A2 as Acceptor A2
    participant A3 as Acceptor A3
    Note over P,A3: Phase 1
    P->>A1: prepare(1)
    P->>A2: prepare(1)
    P-xA3: prepare(1)
    A1-->>P: promise(1), nothing accepted
    A2-->>P: promise(1), nothing accepted
    Note over P,A3: Phase 2
    P->>A1: accept(1, X)
    P->>A2: accept(1, X)
    P-xA3: accept(1, X)
    A1-->>P: accepted(1, X)
    A2-->>P: accepted(1, X)
    Note over P: X chosen by 2 of 3
```

Follow the two round trips: no accept is sent until both promises are back, and X is chosen at the second accepted reply, without A3 ever answering.
@@ verify
import re
class Acceptor:
    def __init__(self): self.promised = 0; self.accepted = None
    def prepare(self, n):
        if n > self.promised:
            self.promised = n
            return self.accepted            # the promise reports what was accepted (None = nothing)
        return 'reject'
    def accept(self, n, v):
        if n >= self.promised:
            self.promised = n; self.accepted = (n, v); return True
        return False
acc = {k: Acceptor() for k in ('A1', 'A2', 'A3')}
up = ['A1', 'A2']
n, own = 1, 'X'
trace = []
for k in acc: trace.append(('P', '->>' if k in up else '-x', k, f'prepare({n})'))
promises = {}
for k in up:
    r = acc[k].prepare(n); assert r != 'reject'; promises[k] = r
    trace.append((k, '-->>', 'P', f'promise({n}), ' + ('nothing accepted' if r is None else f'accepted {r}')))
assert len(promises) > len(acc) // 2
reported = [r for r in promises.values() if r]
v = max(reported)[1] if reported else own
for k in acc: trace.append(('P', '->>' if k in up else '-x', k, f'accept({n}, {v})'))
oks = 0
for k in up:
    assert acc[k].accept(n, v); oks += 1
    trace.append((k, '-->>', 'P', f'accepted({n}, {v})'))
assert v == 'X' and oks == 2 > len(acc) // 2
shown = [m.groups() for m in re.finditer(r'^\s*(\w+)(-->>|->>|-x)(\w+): (.+?)\s*$', block(reply, 'mermaid'), re.M)]
assert [tuple(x) for x in shown] == trace, (shown, trace)
assert 'X chosen by 2 of 3' in reply and '2 of 3' in tutor[0]

@@@@ show-me-circuit-breaker-states
situation: show-me
topic: Circuit breakers
voice: plain
level: easy
expect: {"visual":{"kind":"mermaid","diagram":["stateDiagram"]},"must":["Half-open","Closed","Open"]}
@@ tutor
A circuit breaker wraps the calls to a dependency and is always in one of three states. Closed: calls go through, and the breaker counts consecutive failures. When the count reaches a threshold, say 5 in a row, it trips to open. Open: calls fail at once without touching the dependency, which gives it room to recover and stops callers queuing up on timeouts. After a wait, say 30 s, the breaker moves to half-open. Half-open: one trial call is let through. If it succeeds, the breaker closes and the failure count starts again from zero; if it fails, the breaker opens again and the wait restarts. The threshold and the wait are settings you choose, not fixed parts of the pattern.

<note>Circuit breaker: closed (calls pass, failures counted) → open when the failure threshold is reached (calls fail fast) → half-open after a wait (one trial call) → closed if the trial succeeds, open again if it fails.</note>

<continue/>
@@ learner showme
@@ reply
```mermaid
stateDiagram-v2
    state "Half-open" as HalfOpen
    [*] --> Closed
    Closed --> Open: 5 failures in a row
    Open --> HalfOpen: 30 s wait is over
    HalfOpen --> Closed: trial call succeeds
    HalfOpen --> Open: trial call fails
```

Notice that no arrow leads from Open straight back to Closed: the only way to close again is through a successful trial call in Half-open.
@@ verify
import re
edges = {(a, b): label for a, b, label in re.findall(r'^\s*(\w+) --> (\w+): (.+?)\s*$', block(reply, 'mermaid'), re.M)}
assert set(edges) == {('Closed', 'Open'), ('Open', 'HalfOpen'), ('HalfOpen', 'Closed'), ('HalfOpen', 'Open')}, edges
assert '[*] --> Closed' in reply and ('Open', 'Closed') not in edges
# an independent breaker, driven through a script; every transition it makes must be an arrow in the diagram
class Breaker:
    def __init__(self, threshold=5, wait=30):
        self.state, self.fails, self.opened, self.threshold, self.wait = 'Closed', 0, None, threshold, wait
        self.moves = []
    def _go(self, to, why): self.moves.append((self.state, to, why)); self.state = to
    def call(self, now, ok):
        if self.state == 'Open':
            if now - self.opened < self.wait: return 'fast-fail'
            self._go('HalfOpen', 'wait')
        if self.state == 'HalfOpen':
            if ok: self.fails = 0; self._go('Closed', 'success')
            else: self.opened = now; self._go('Open', 'failure')
            return ok
        self.fails = 0 if ok else self.fails + 1
        if self.fails >= self.threshold: self.opened = now; self._go('Open', 'threshold')
        return ok
b = Breaker()
for t in range(4): b.call(t, False)
assert b.state == 'Closed'                      # 4 failures: still closed
b.call(4, False); assert b.state == 'Open'      # the 5th in a row trips it
assert b.call(20, True) == 'fast-fail' and b.state == 'Open'
b.call(34, False); assert b.state == 'Open'     # trial after 30 s fails: open again, wait restarts
assert b.call(50, True) == 'fast-fail'
b.call(64, True); assert b.state == 'Closed' and b.fails == 0
assert {(a, c) for a, c, _ in b.moves} == set(edges)
assert '5 failures in a row' in edges[('Closed', 'Open')] and '5 in a row' in tutor[0]
assert '30 s' in edges[('Open', 'HalfOpen')] and '30 s' in tutor[0]

@@@@ show-me-read-repair-flow
situation: show-me
topic: Hinted handoff and read repair
voice: plain
level: medium
expect: {"visual":{"kind":"mermaid","diagram":["flowchart"]},"must":["version 7","version 5"],"ends":"either","askTypes":["short","predict"],"askMustNot":["stale|out of date","nothing (is|would be|gets) repaired","no repair"]}
@@ tutor
Read repair fixes stale replicas as a side effect of ordinary reads. The coordinator sends the read to several replicas and compares the versions that come back. It gives the client the newest value, and writes that value back to any replica that returned an older one.

An example: a key is stored on replicas R1, R2 and R3. R3 was down during the last write, so R1 and R2 hold version 7 and R3 still holds version 5. A read gets 7, 7 and 5 back. The client is given version 7, and the coordinator sends version 7 to R3. A key that is never read is never repaired this way, which is why these stores also run a background anti-entropy process.

<note>Read repair: on a read, the coordinator compares the versions returned by the replicas, answers with the newest, and writes it back to the replicas that were behind. It only repairs keys that are read.</note>

<continue/>
@@ learner question
I'd like to see that read as a diagram, with the three replicas in it.
@@ reply
```mermaid
flowchart LR
    C["Client"] -->|"1 read key"| K["Coordinator"]
    K -->|"2 read"| R1["R1: version 7"]
    K -->|"2 read"| R2["R2: version 7"]
    K -->|"2 read"| R3["R3: version 5"]
    K -->|"3 newest is version 7"| C
    K -->|"4 write version 7"| R3
```

Step 4 is the repair: it goes only to R3, the one replica whose reply was older than the newest.

<ask type="predict">Suppose the coordinator had sent this read to R3 alone. What would the client have been given, and what would have happened to R3's copy?</ask>
@@ verify
import re
# the read, simulated: versions held, newest returned, older replicas written back
held = {'R1': 7, 'R2': 7, 'R3': 5}
replies = dict(held)
newest = max(replies.values())
repaired = [r for r, v in replies.items() if v < newest]
for r in repaired: held[r] = newest
assert newest == 7 and repaired == ['R3'] and held == {'R1': 7, 'R2': 7, 'R3': 7}
d = block(reply, 'mermaid')
for r, v in replies.items():
    assert f'{r}["{r}: version {v}"]' in d, r
assert f'newest is version {newest}"| C' in d
writes = re.findall(r'K -->\|"4 write version (\d+)"\| (\w+)', d)
assert writes == [('7', 'R3')], writes
assert len(re.findall(r'K -->\|"2 read"\|', d)) == 3
# the question's case: R3 alone returns 5 and there is nothing to compare it with
alone = {'R3': 5}
assert max(alone.values()) == 5 and [r for r, v in alone.items() if v < max(alone.values())] == []

@@@@ show-me-lamport-trace-table
situation: show-me
topic: Lamport logical clocks
voice: plain
level: easy
expect: {"visual":{"kind":"table"},"must":["max\\(3, 4\\) \\+ 1"]}
@@ tutor
Trace the rules on two processes, A and B, both starting at 0. A has a local event (A = 1), then sends message m to B (A = 2, and m carries 2). Meanwhile B has a local event (B = 1). B receives m: max(1, 2) + 1 = 3. B then sends message k to A (B = 4, and k carries 4). Before k arrives, A has another local event (A = 3). A receives k: max(3, 4) + 1 = 5.

<note>Lamport clock rules: on a local event or a send, clock + 1. On a receive, max(own clock, message timestamp) + 1.</note>

<continue/>
@@ learner showme
@@ reply
| Step | Event | Rule | A's clock | B's clock |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | A: local event | 0 + 1 | 1 | 0 |
| 2 | A: send m (carries 2) | 1 + 1 | 2 | 0 |
| 3 | B: local event | 0 + 1 | 2 | 1 |
| 4 | B: receive m | max(1, 2) + 1 | 2 | 3 |
| 5 | B: send k (carries 4) | 3 + 1 | 2 | 4 |
| 6 | A: local event | 2 + 1 | 3 | 4 |
| 7 | A: receive k | max(3, 4) + 1 | 5 | 4 |

Compare rows 4 and 7 with the others: only a receive can make a clock jump by more than one, and it always lands above the timestamp the message carried.
@@ verify
clock = {'A': 0, 'B': 0}
msgs = {}
script = [('A', 'local', None), ('A', 'send', 'm'), ('B', 'local', None), ('B', 'recv', 'm'), ('B', 'send', 'k'), ('A', 'local', None), ('A', 'recv', 'k')]
want = []
for i, (p, kind, m) in enumerate(script, 1):
    before = clock[p]
    if kind == 'recv':
        rule = f'max({before}, {msgs[m]}) + 1'
        clock[p] = max(before, msgs[m]) + 1
        event = f'{p}: receive {m}'
    else:
        rule = f'{before} + 1'
        clock[p] = before + 1
        if kind == 'send':
            msgs[m] = clock[p]
            event = f'{p}: send {m} (carries {clock[p]})'
        else:
            event = f'{p}: local event'
    want.append([str(i), event, rule, str(clock['A']), str(clock['B'])])
rows = table(reply)[1:]
assert rows == want, (rows, want)
assert clock == {'A': 5, 'B': 3 + 1} and msgs == {'m': 2, 'k': 4}
# the explanation states the same values
for s in ('A = 1', 'A = 2', 'm carries 2', 'B = 1', 'max(1, 2) + 1 = 3', 'B = 4', 'k carries 4', 'A = 3', 'max(3, 4) + 1 = 5'):
    assert s in tutor[0], s
# receives are the only jumps of more than one, and each lands above the carried timestamp
assert 3 - 1 > 1 and 5 - 3 > 1 and 3 > 2 and 5 > 4

@@@@ mark-error-availability-minutes
situation: mark-error
topic: Availability arithmetic: nines, series and parallel
voice: wordy
level: medium
expect: {"verdict":"partial","accept":["wrong"],"names":["4,320","43,200"],"must":["43,200","about 69 minutes"],"setsProblem":true,"askTypes":["problem"],"askMustNot":["13\\.1"]}
@@ tutor
**Problem 3 · harder**

A request passes through a load balancer (99.99% available), an application tier (99.9%) and a database (99.95%), in series, failing independently. What is the availability of the whole path, and how many minutes of downtime does that allow in a 30-day month?

<solution>0.9999 × 0.999 × 0.9995 = 0.9984 (99.84%), so the path is unavailable 0.0016 of the time. A 30-day month has 30 × 24 × 60 = 43,200 minutes, and 0.0016 × 43,200 is about 69 minutes.</solution>
<check lang="python">
a = 0.9999 * 0.999 * 0.9995
assert round(a, 4) == 0.9984
assert 30 * 24 * 60 == 43200 and round((1 - a) * 43200) == 69
</check>

<ask type="problem">The availability and the downtime in minutes, with your working.</ask>
@@ learner answer sure
It's in series, so everything has to be up at once and I multiply: 0.9999 × 0.999 × 0.9995 = 0.9984, so 99.84%. That leaves 1 − 0.9984 = 0.0016 unavailable. A 30 day month is 30 × 24 × 60 = 4,320 minutes. So the downtime is 0.0016 × 4,320 = 6.9 minutes a month.
@@ reply
<grade verdict="partial" score="0.6">The method and the first two steps are right: "0.9999 × 0.999 × 0.9995 = 0.9984" and "1 − 0.9984 = 0.0016 unavailable". The first wrong step is "30 × 24 × 60 = 4,320 minutes": 30 × 24 = 720 hours and 720 × 60 = 43,200 minutes. Your 6.9 follows correctly from the wrong minute count; with 43,200 the downtime is 0.0016 × 43,200, about 69 minutes a month.</grade>

A quick check for this kind of figure: 99.9% alone allows 43.2 minutes in a 30-day month, and this path is a little worse than 99.9%, so an answer under 43 minutes cannot be right.

<card>How many minutes are in a 30-day month, and how much downtime does 99.9% allow in it? :: 30 × 24 × 60 = 43,200 minutes; 0.1% of that is 43.2 minutes.</card>

**Problem 4 · same level**

An API depends, in series, on an authentication service (99.95% available) and a payments service (99.9%), failing independently. What is the availability of the API, and how many hours of downtime does that allow in a 365-day year?

<solution>0.9995 × 0.999 = 0.9985 (99.85%), unavailable 0.0015 of the time. A year has 365 × 24 = 8,760 hours, and 0.0015 × 8,760 is about 13.1 hours.</solution>
<check lang="python">
a = 0.9995 * 0.999
assert round(a, 4) == 0.9985
assert 365 * 24 == 8760 and round((1 - a) * 8760, 1) == 13.1
</check>

<ask type="problem">The availability and the downtime in hours, with the number of hours you used for the year.</ask>
@@ verify
a = 0.9999 * 0.999 * 0.9995
assert round(a, 4) == 0.9984 and round(1 - 0.9984, 4) == 0.0016
assert 30 * 24 == 720 and 720 * 60 == 43200 != 4320
assert round(0.0016 * 4320, 1) == 6.9                    # the learner's figure follows from their minute count
assert round(0.0016 * 43200) == 69 and round((1 - a) * 43200) == 69
assert abs(0.001 * 43200 - 43.2) < 1e-9 and a < 0.999    # the sanity check in the reply
b = 0.9995 * 0.999
assert round(b, 4) == 0.9985 and round(0.0015 * 8760, 1) == 13.1 and round((1 - b) * 8760, 1) == 13.1
assert '13.1' in solution(reply) and '13.1' not in visible(reply)

@@@@ mark-error-token-bucket-over-capacity
situation: mark-error
topic: Rate limiting with a token bucket
voice: typos
level: medium
expect: {"verdict":"partial","accept":["wrong"],"names":["1 \\+ 8 = 9","capacity|at most 5|capped"],"must":["\\b11\\b"],"setsProblem":true,"askTypes":["problem"],"askMustNot":["\\b10 of\\b","10 are admitted"]}
@@ tutor
**Problem 2 · harder**

A token bucket has capacity 5 and gains 1 token each second, up to its capacity. It starts full. Requests arrive in groups: 3 at t = 0 s, 2 at t = 1 s, 8 at t = 9 s and 2 at t = 10 s. Each request takes one token, or is rejected if there is none. How many of the 15 requests are admitted?

<solution>t = 0: 5 tokens, admit 3, 2 left. t = 1: 3 tokens, admit 2, 1 left. t = 9: 1 + 8 would be 9, capped at 5; admit 5, reject 3, 0 left. t = 10: 1 token, admit 1, reject 1. Admitted: 3 + 2 + 5 + 1 = 11.</solution>
<check lang="python">
tokens, last, admitted = 5, 0, 0
for t, n in [(0, 3), (1, 2), (9, 8), (10, 2)]:
    tokens = min(5, tokens + (t - last)); last = t
    ok = min(tokens, n); tokens -= ok; admitted += ok
assert admitted == 11
</check>

<ask type="problem">The number admitted, with the token count at each arrival.</ask>
@@ learner answer sure
t=0: bucket has 5, the 3 go through, 2 left.
t=1: one token added so 3, the 2 go through, 1 left.
t=9: 8 seconds have pased so 1 + 8 = 9 tokens, all 8 go thru, 1 left.
t=10: one more token so 2, both go thru.
Total 3 + 2 + 8 + 2 = 15, so all 15 are admited.
@@ reply
<grade verdict="partial" score="0.6">The first two steps are right: "the 3 go through, 2 left" and "one token added so 3, the 2 go through, 1 left". The first wrong step is "1 + 8 = 9 tokens": the bucket holds at most its capacity of 5, so the extra tokens are discarded. At t = 9 it has 5, admits 5 of the 8 and rejects 3, leaving 0.</grade>

Your last line follows correctly from the 1 token you thought was left; it is not a second error. From 0 left, t = 10 has 1 token and admits 1 of the 2. The total is 3 + 2 + 5 + 1 = 11 of 15.

<card>A token bucket of capacity 5 holds 1 token and then sits idle for 8 seconds at 1 token a second. How many tokens does it hold? :: 5, not 9. Refill stops at the capacity: tokens = min(capacity, tokens + rate × time).</card>

**Problem 3 · same level**

A token bucket has capacity 4 and gains 2 tokens each second, up to its capacity. It starts full. Requests arrive in groups: 4 at t = 0 s, 3 at t = 1 s and 9 at t = 6 s. How many of the 16 requests are admitted?

<solution>t = 0: 4 tokens, admit 4, 0 left. t = 1: 2 tokens, admit 2, reject 1, 0 left. t = 6: 0 + 10 would be 10, capped at 4; admit 4, reject 5. Admitted: 4 + 2 + 4 = 10.</solution>
<check lang="python">
tokens, last, admitted = 4, 0, 0
for t, n in [(0, 4), (1, 3), (6, 9)]:
    tokens = min(4, tokens + 2 * (t - last)); last = t
    ok = min(tokens, n); tokens -= ok; admitted += ok
assert admitted == 10
</check>

<ask type="problem">The number admitted, with the token count just before each group arrives.</ask>
@@ verify
def run(capacity, rate, arrivals, capped=True):
    tokens, last, steps = capacity, 0, []
    for t, n in arrivals:
        tokens = tokens + rate * (t - last)
        if capped: tokens = min(capacity, tokens)
        last = t
        ok = min(tokens, n); steps.append((tokens, ok, tokens - ok)); tokens -= ok
    return steps
arr = [(0, 3), (1, 2), (9, 8), (10, 2)]
right = run(5, 1, arr)
assert right == [(5, 3, 2), (3, 2, 1), (5, 5, 0), (1, 1, 0)] and sum(s[1] for s in right) == 11
# the learner's trace is exactly the same bucket with the cap left out: one slip, the rest follows
theirs = run(5, 1, arr, capped=False)
assert theirs == [(5, 3, 2), (3, 2, 1), (9, 8, 1), (2, 2, 0)] and sum(s[1] for s in theirs) == 15
assert right[:2] == theirs[:2]
p3 = run(4, 2, [(0, 4), (1, 3), (6, 9)])
assert p3 == [(4, 4, 0), (2, 2, 0), (4, 4, 0)] and sum(s[1] for s in p3) == 10
assert 'Admitted: 4 + 2 + 4 = 10' in solution(reply) and '4 + 2 + 4' not in visible(reply)

@@@@ mark-error-raft-commit-followers-only
situation: mark-error
topic: Raft log replication and the commit index
voice: confused
level: medium
expect: {"verdict":"partial","accept":["wrong"],"names":["3 followers|three followers","leader"],"must":["commit index is 10|commit index of 10"],"setsProblem":true,"askTypes":["problem"],"askMustNot":["\\b19\\b"]}
@@ tutor
**Problem 2 · harder**

A Raft cluster has 5 servers. The leader's last log index is 12, and its matchIndex values for the four followers are 9, 12, 7 and 10. Every entry is from the leader's current term. What is the commit index?

<solution>The five servers hold up to 12 (leader), 12, 10, 9 and 7. A majority of 5 is 3, and the third highest is 10: index 10 is stored on the leader and two followers. Index 11 is on only 2 servers. The commit index is 10.</solution>
<check lang="python">
idx = [12, 9, 12, 7, 10]
need = len(idx) // 2 + 1
assert max(n for n in range(13) if sum(i >= n for i in idx) >= need) == 10
</check>

<ask type="problem">The commit index, with the servers that hold that entry.</ask>
@@ learner answer unsure
ok so majority of 5 is 3. followers are at 9, 12, 7, 10. so I need 3 followers that have the entry. index 12 is on one follower, index 10 is on two followers (the 12 one and the 10 one), index 9 is on three (12, 10, 9). so commit index = 9? I think
@@ reply
<grade verdict="partial" score="0.5">"majority of 5 is 3" is right, and so are your counts of followers at each index. The first wrong step is "I need 3 followers": the majority is 3 of the 5 servers, and the leader is one of them, so 2 followers plus the leader is enough.</grade>

The leader holds every entry up to 12, so add it to each of your counts. Index 10 is on "two followers", which makes 3 servers with the leader: a majority. Index 11 is on one follower plus the leader, 2 servers, which is not. So the commit index is 10, not 9. Your 9 follows correctly from the count you set yourself; the count was the only slip.

<card>When a Raft leader counts replicas to commit an entry, does it count itself? :: Yes. A majority is of all servers, leader included: in a 5-server cluster, the leader plus 2 followers.</card>

<note>The majority for commitment is counted over all servers. The leader always holds its own entries, so it needs the entry on (majority − 1) followers.</note>

**Problem 3 · same level**

Another 5-server cluster. The leader's last log index is 21, and its matchIndex values for the four followers are 21, 16, 19 and 13. Every entry is from the current term. What is the commit index?

<solution>The five servers hold up to 21 (leader), 21, 19, 16 and 13. The third highest is 19: index 19 is on the leader and two followers, while 20 and 21 are on only 2 servers. The commit index is 19.</solution>
<check lang="python">
idx = [21, 21, 16, 19, 13]
need = len(idx) // 2 + 1
assert max(n for n in range(22) if sum(i >= n for i in idx) >= need) == 19
</check>

<ask type="problem">The commit index, counting the leader.</ask>
@@ verify
def commit(indices, need):
    return max(n for n in range(max(indices) + 1) if sum(i >= n for i in indices) >= need)
followers, leader = [9, 12, 7, 10], 12
assert commit(followers + [leader], 3) == 10
# the learner's counts of followers at each index, and the answer their rule gives
at = lambda n: sum(i >= n for i in followers)
assert (at(12), at(10), at(9)) == (1, 2, 3)
assert commit(followers, 3) == 9                         # three followers required: their 9
assert at(10) + 1 == 3 and at(11) + 1 == 2               # with the leader counted
assert commit([21, 16, 19, 13] + [21], 3) == 19
assert sorted([21, 21, 16, 19, 13], reverse=True)[2] == 19
assert 'commit index is 19' in solution(reply) and '19' not in visible(reply).split('Problem 3')[1].replace('16, 19 and 13', '')

@@@@ mark-error-backoff-cap-forgotten
situation: mark-error
topic: Retries with exponential backoff and jitter
