assert waits[-1] == 5000 and sum(waits) == 11200
</check>

<ask type="problem">The total wait before retry 6, with the list of waits.</ask>
@@ learner answer sure
waits are 200, 400, 800, 1600, 3200 and then 6400 for the 6th. total = 200+400+800+1600+3200+6400 = 12600 ms so 12.6 secodns
@@ reply
<grade verdict="partial" score="0.6">The doubling is right as far as "200, 400, 800, 1600, 3200". The first wrong step is "then 6400 for the 6th": the waits are capped at 5 s, and 6,400 ms is over the cap, so the sixth wait is 5,000 ms. Your sum is added correctly; with the capped value it is 6,200 + 5,000 = 11,200 ms, or 11.2 s.</grade>

<card>How is a capped exponential backoff wait computed? :: wait = min(cap, base × 2^(retry − 1)). Once doubling passes the cap, every later wait equals the cap.</card>

**Problem 3 · a little harder**

A client waits 500 ms before retry 1, doubling each time, capped at 4 s, with no jitter. It gives up if retry 5 fails. How long has it waited in total when it makes retry 5?

<solution>The waits are 500, 1,000, 2,000 and 4,000 ms, then 8,000 ms capped to 4,000 ms. Total 11,500 ms = 11.5 s.</solution>
<check lang="python">
waits = [min(4000, 500 * 2 ** i) for i in range(5)]
assert waits == [500, 1000, 2000, 4000, 4000] and sum(waits) == 11500
</check>

<ask type="problem">The total wait before retry 5, with the wait before each retry.</ask>
@@ verify
capped = [min(5000, 200 * 2 ** i) for i in range(6)]
plain = [200 * 2 ** i for i in range(6)]
assert plain == [200, 400, 800, 1600, 3200, 6400] and sum(plain) == 12600      # the learner's list and sum
assert capped == [200, 400, 800, 1600, 3200, 5000] and sum(capped) == 11200
assert sum(capped[:5]) == 6200 and 6200 + 5000 == 11200
assert plain[:5] == capped[:5]                                                 # one slip only: the sixth wait
w = [min(4000, 500 * 2 ** i) for i in range(5)]
assert w == [500, 1000, 2000, 4000, 4000] and sum(w) == 11500
assert '11,500' in solution(reply) and '11,500' not in visible(reply)

@@@@ unusual-availability-inclusion-exclusion
situation: mark-unusual
topic: Availability arithmetic: nines, series and parallel
voice: terse
level: medium
expect: {"verdict":"correct","mustNot":["should have used","the standard (way|method) is","instead you should"],"must":["99\\.95%|0\\.9995"],"setsProblem":true,"askTypes":["problem"],"askMustNot":["0\\.999\\b","99\\.9%"]}
@@ tutor
**Problem 4 · harder**

A database has two replicas in different zones, and a read succeeds if at least one of them is up. One replica is up 99% of the time and the other 95%, independently. What is the availability of reads?

<solution>Reads fail only when both replicas are down: 0.01 × 0.05 = 0.0005. Availability = 1 − 0.0005 = 0.9995, which is 99.95%.</solution>
<check lang="python">
assert abs(1 - 0.01 * 0.05 - 0.9995) < 1e-12
</check>

<ask type="problem">The availability as a percentage, with your working.</ask>
@@ learner answer sure
P(A) + P(B) − P(both up) = 0.99 + 0.95 − 0.99 × 0.95 = 1.94 − 0.9405 = 0.9995. 99.95%
@@ reply
<grade verdict="correct" score="1">"P(A) + P(B) − P(both up)" is inclusion-exclusion for "at least one is up", and with independent replicas P(both up) is 0.99 × 0.95 = 0.9405, so 0.9995 is right. It is the same quantity as 1 − 0.01 × 0.05: expand $1 - (1 - a)(1 - b)$ and you get $a + b - ab$.</grade>

Your method works for any two components. With three or more, inclusion-exclusion grows extra terms, so keep an eye on which route is shorter.

**Problem 5 · harder**

Three replicas, each up 90% of the time and failing independently; a read needs at least one. What is the availability of reads?

<solution>All three down: 0.1 × 0.1 × 0.1 = 0.001. Availability = 1 − 0.001 = 0.999, which is 99.9%. By inclusion-exclusion: 3(0.9) − 3(0.81) + 0.729 = 2.7 − 2.43 + 0.729 = 0.999.</solution>
<check lang="python">
a = 0.9
assert abs(1 - (1 - a) ** 3 - 0.999) < 1e-12
assert abs(3 * a - 3 * a * a + a ** 3 - 0.999) < 1e-12
</check>

<ask type="problem">The availability as a percentage, by whichever method you prefer.</ask>
@@ verify
import itertools
assert abs(0.99 * 0.95 - 0.9405) < 1e-12 and abs(0.99 + 0.95 - 1.94) < 1e-12
assert abs(1.94 - 0.9405 - 0.9995) < 1e-12                 # the learner's arithmetic, step by step
assert abs(1 - 0.01 * 0.05 - 0.9995) < 1e-12               # the standard route gives the same
# the identity the grade states, checked on a grid
for a in (0.5, 0.9, 0.99):
    for b in (0.3, 0.95, 0.999):
        assert abs((1 - (1 - a) * (1 - b)) - (a + b - a * b)) < 1e-12
def at_least_one(ps):
    return sum(__import__('math').prod(p if up else 1 - p for p, up in zip(ps, ups)) for ups in itertools.product([0, 1], repeat=len(ps)) if any(ups))
assert abs(at_least_one([0.99, 0.95]) - 0.9995) < 1e-12
assert abs(at_least_one([0.9] * 3) - 0.999) < 1e-12
assert abs(2.7 - 2.43 + 0.729 - 0.999) < 1e-12
assert '0.999' in solution(reply) and '0.999' not in visible(reply).replace('0.9995', '')

@@@@ unusual-raft-commit-count-down
situation: mark-unusual
topic: Raft log replication and the commit index
options: {"difficulty":"hard"}
voice: terse
level: hard
expect: {"verdict":"correct","mustNot":["should have (used|sorted)","you (need|have) to sort"],"must":["4 of 7|4 servers"],"setsProblem":true,"askTypes":["problem"],"askMustNot":["stays at 6","does not (move|advance)","cannot (be committed|advance)"]}
@@ tutor
**Problem 2 · harder**

A Raft cluster has 7 servers. The leader's last log index is 20, and its matchIndex values for the six followers are 18, 20, 15, 17, 11 and 19. Every entry is from the leader's current term. What is the commit index?

<solution>The seven servers hold up to 20 (leader), 20, 19, 18, 17, 15 and 11. A majority of 7 is 4, and the fourth highest is 18. Index 19 is on only 3 servers. The commit index is 18.</solution>
<check lang="python">
idx = [20, 18, 20, 15, 17, 11, 19]
need = len(idx) // 2 + 1
assert max(n for n in range(21) if sum(i >= n for i in idx) >= need) == 18
</check>

<ask type="problem">The commit index, with your reasoning.</ask>
@@ learner answer sure
18. went down from the top counting servers incl. the leader: index 20 is on 2, index 19 is on 3, index 18 is on 4. 4 of 7 is a majority so stop there.
@@ reply
<grade verdict="correct" score="1">"index 20 is on 2, index 19 is on 3, index 18 is on 4" applies the definition directly: the commit index is the highest index held by a majority, and 4 of 7 is the first majority you meet coming down from the top. Taking the fourth highest of the sorted indices gives the same 18, for the same reason.</grade>

**Problem 3 · harder**

A 5-server cluster. The leader is in term 4 and its last log index is 9. Its matchIndex values for the four followers are 9, 8, 8 and 5. Entries up to index 8 were written in term 3; only index 9 is from term 4. The commit index is currently 6. What is the commit index after the leader re-evaluates it?

<solution>Index 8 is on 4 of 5 servers, but its entry is from term 3, and a Raft leader never commits an entry from an earlier term by counting replicas. Index 9 is from term 4 but is on only 2 servers (the leader and one follower). So the commit index stays at 6. When index 9 reaches a third server it commits, and indices 7 and 8 are committed with it.</solution>
<check lang="python">
idx = [9, 9, 8, 8, 5]
term = {n: 3 for n in range(1, 9)}; term[9] = 4
need, current, commit = 3, 4, 6
for n in range(commit + 1, 10):
    if sum(i >= n for i in idx) >= need and term[n] == current:
        commit = n
assert commit == 6
</check>

<ask type="problem">The commit index, with the reason for each index you considered.</ask>
@@ verify
idx = [20, 18, 20, 15, 17, 11, 19]
on = lambda n: sum(i >= n for i in idx)
assert (on(20), on(19), on(18)) == (2, 3, 4) and len(idx) // 2 + 1 == 4          # the learner's counts
assert sorted(idx, reverse=True)[3] == 18                                         # the sorting route agrees
# problem 3, as the leader's rule: majority AND current term
idx3 = [9, 9, 8, 8, 5]
term = {n: 3 for n in range(1, 9)}; term[9] = 4
on3 = lambda n: sum(i >= n for i in idx3)
assert on3(8) == 4 and on3(9) == 2 and term[8] == 3 and term[9] == 4
commit = 6
for n in range(7, 10):
    if on3(n) >= 3 and term[n] == 4: commit = n
assert commit == 6
# once a third server stores index 9, it commits and carries 7 and 8 with it
idx3b = [9, 9, 9, 8, 5]
assert sum(i >= 9 for i in idx3b) == 3
assert 'stays at 6' in solution(reply) and 'stays at 6' not in visible(reply)

@@@@ unusual-ntp-offset-by-delay
situation: mark-unusual
topic: Clock skew and NTP
voice: wordy
level: medium
expect: {"verdict":"correct","mustNot":["should have used","the formula you should"],"must":["0\\.100|100 ms","symmetric|same in both directions|each way"],"setsProblem":true,"askTypes":["problem"],"askMustNot":["0\\.080","80 ms","0\\.020","20 ms"]}
@@ tutor
NTP estimates how far a client's clock is from a server's using four timestamps: the client sends at $t_0$ and receives the reply at $t_3$ (client clock); the server receives at $t_1$ and replies at $t_2$ (server clock). Offset $= \frac{(t_1 - t_0) + (t_2 - t_3)}{2}$.

<note>NTP offset $= \frac{(t_1 - t_0) + (t_2 - t_3)}{2}$, positive when the client's clock is behind. It is exact only if the network delay is the same in both directions.</note>

**Problem 1 · warm-up**

A client sends a request at $t_0 = 10.000$ s. The server receives it at $t_1 = 10.130$ s and replies at $t_2 = 10.134$ s. The client receives the reply at $t_3 = 10.064$ s. Assume the delay is the same in both directions. How far off is the client's clock, and in which direction?

<solution>Offset = ((10.130 − 10.000) + (10.134 − 10.064)) / 2 = (0.130 + 0.070) / 2 = 0.100 s. The client's clock is 100 ms behind the server's.</solution>
<check lang="python">
t0, t1, t2, t3 = 10.000, 10.130, 10.134, 10.064
assert abs(((t1 - t0) + (t2 - t3)) / 2 - 0.100) < 1e-9
</check>

<ask type="problem">The offset and its direction, with your working.</ask>
@@ learner answer sure
I did it a different way, hope that's ok. The round trip on my clock is 10.064 − 10.000 = 0.064 s, and the server sat on the request for 0.004 s, so the network took 0.060 s in total, which is 0.030 each way. The server stamped its reply 10.134, so when the reply reached me the server's clock must have read 10.134 + 0.030 = 10.164. Mine read 10.064. So my clock is 0.100 s behind.
@@ reply
<grade verdict="correct" score="1">"when the reply reached me the server's clock must have read 10.134 + 0.030 = 10.164" is sound: you found the network delay first, 0.060 s split as "0.030 each way", then compared the two clocks at one instant. The result, 0.100 s behind, is right.</grade>

Your route and the formula rest on the same assumption, a symmetric delay, and they are the same algebra: $t_2 + \frac{(t_3 - t_0) - (t_2 - t_1)}{2} - t_3$ rearranges to $\frac{(t_1 - t_0) + (t_2 - t_3)}{2}$. Yours has the advantage of producing the round-trip delay on the way, which NTP also reports.

**Problem 2 · harder**

Keep the same four timestamps, but suppose the true network delays were 0.050 s on the way out and 0.010 s on the way back. What is the client's true offset, and how far out was the estimate of 0.100 s?

<solution>The request left at 10.000 on the client's clock and arrived 0.050 s later, when the server read 10.130, so the server is ahead by 10.130 − 10.050 = 0.080 s. Check with the reply: 10.134 − 0.080 + 0.010 = 10.064. The true offset is 0.080 s, so the estimate is out by 0.020 s, which is half the difference between the two delays: (0.050 − 0.010) / 2.</solution>
<check lang="python">
t0, t1, t2, t3 = 10.000, 10.130, 10.134, 10.064
out, back = 0.050, 0.010
true = t1 - (t0 + out)
assert abs(true - 0.080) < 1e-9 and abs((t2 - true + back) - t3) < 1e-9
est = ((t1 - t0) + (t2 - t3)) / 2
assert abs((est - true) - (out - back) / 2) < 1e-9 and abs(est - true - 0.020) < 1e-9
</check>

<ask type="problem">The true offset and the error of the estimate, with your working.</ask>
@@ verify
t0, t1, t2, t3 = 10.000, 10.130, 10.134, 10.064
ap = lambda x, y: abs(x - y) < 1e-9
# the learner's route, step by step
rt = t3 - t0; hold = t2 - t1; net = rt - hold
assert ap(rt, 0.064) and ap(hold, 0.004) and ap(net, 0.060) and ap(net / 2, 0.030)
assert ap(t2 + net / 2, 10.164) and ap(t2 + net / 2 - t3, 0.100)
# the formula gives the same, and the two are the same algebra for arbitrary timestamps
assert ap(((t1 - t0) + (t2 - t3)) / 2, 0.100)
import random
rng = random.Random(4)
for _ in range(200):
    a, b, c, d = sorted(rng.uniform(0, 5) for _ in range(4))
    q0, q3 = a, d; q1, q2 = b + rng.uniform(-2, 2), 0
    q2 = q1 + (c - b)
    assert ap(q2 + ((q3 - q0) - (q2 - q1)) / 2 - q3, ((q1 - q0) + (q2 - q3)) / 2)
# a ground-truth simulation of problem 1: true offset 0.100, delays 0.030 each way, server holds 0.004
off, dly = 0.100, 0.030
s1 = t0 + dly + off; s2 = s1 + 0.004; c3 = s2 - off + dly
assert ap(s1, t1) and ap(s2, t2) and ap(c3, t3)
# problem 2: the same timestamps arise from offset 0.080 with delays 0.050 and 0.010
off, out, back = 0.080, 0.050, 0.010
s1 = t0 + out + off; s2 = s1 + 0.004; c3 = s2 - off + back
assert ap(s1, t1) and ap(s2, t2) and ap(c3, t3)
assert ap(0.100 - 0.080, (out - back) / 2)
assert '0.080' in solution(reply) and '0.080' not in visible(reply)
